/**
 * CCA-F vocabulary deck - terms pulled from the ten mock exams and the Exam Guide.
 *
 * Hardcoded rather than stored in the database: this is reference content that only
 * changes when a new exam attempt surfaces new terms, which is a code change (a new
 * entry appended below), not a content-editing workflow that needs an admin form.
 *
 * Each item is `[term, meaning (vi), part of speech, example, example translation]`. A
 * tuple instead of an object keeps 290 entries scannable in one screen - a labelled
 * object per row would triple the line count for no information gain.
 *
 * ## Topics follow the exam's domains, subdivided to stay studiable
 *
 * The ten groups below are driven by the five scored CCA-F domains, but the two domains
 * that dominate the deck are split so no topic is a hundred-word wall: Agentic becomes
 * loop/orchestration, multi-agent/synthesis and action-safety; Tool/MCP splits into schema
 * design and error-and-ops; Prompt/Output splits into prompt craft and structured output.
 * A tenth group holds the business-scenario words (refund, warranty, ledger) that every
 * domain's questions lean on without any of them owning.
 *
 * They used to be fourteen groups carrying the shape of how the deck was assembled -
 * "Bổ sung - Đề 1", "Bổ sung - Đề 3-9" - which said where a word was found rather than
 * what it belongs to, and split Agentic Architecture across three separate lists.
 *
 * ## Every example is a real exam sentence
 *
 * The `ex` field quotes the question bank verbatim wherever the term appears there, so
 * the phrasing studied here is the phrasing the exam uses. The 92 entries that once
 * carried a Vietnamese gloss in that slot, with no English at all and no translation
 * below it, now carry both.
 */

export type VocabGroup = {
  cat: string
  items: [term: string, vi: string, pos: string, ex: string, exVi: string][]
}

